  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-115225",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1}+1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1} & >-1 \\\\ {7}^{3x-1} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $7>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${7}^{3x-1}$ is greater than $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>-2\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
